          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which alarm features in reflux does guidance say should change the plan?",
          "answer": "Guidance on gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) indicates specific \"alarm features\" that should prompt changes in management plans. The following features signal the need for prompt endoscopy rather than an empiric trial of treatment:\n\n1. **Difficulty swallowing or pain on swallowing.**\n2. **Unintentional weight loss.**\n3. **Gastrointestinal bleeding** — for example, vomiting blood or black tarry stools.\n4. **Iron-deficiency anemia.**\n5. **Persistent vomiting.**\n6. **A palpable abdominal mass or a family history of upper gastrointestinal cancer.**\n7. **New onset of symptoms at an older age.**\n\nThese alarm features suggest a potentially serious underlying condition, warranting further investigation ([Gastroenterology society clinical guideline](https://www.gastro.org/guidelines/gerd)).\n\nFor most adults with typical GERD symptoms and no alarm features, initial management may involve an empiric eight-week trial of acid suppression with a proton pump inhibitor, with reassessment afterward. The presence of alarm features, however, changes this approach to prioritize immediate diagnostic evaluation ([General clinical reference](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559141/)). \n\nThus, when these alarm features are present, clinicians are directed to pursue more aggressive diagnostic measures to rule out serious complications or conditions.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
